data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies organisms' genomes and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 nuclease, complexed with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), is delivered into a cell so its genome can be cut at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing targets insertions to site-specific locations, using programmable molecules called \"molecular scissors\" to create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences are derived from past bacteriophage DNA and help detect and destroy similar phage DNA during later infections [2].\n- Headlines report CRISPR opening the door to treating many diseases [4] and HIV in cell culture being completely eliminated with CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

