data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"analyst","content":"- **Natural origin:** CRISPR-Cas9 editing adapts a bacterial antiviral defense, in which Cas9 paired with a guide RNA cuts DNA at a targeted site [1]. Bacteria similarly use CRISPR sequences from past phage DNA to detect and destroy returning phages [2].\n\n- **Programmable precision:** Guided \"molecular scissors\" create double-strand breaks, enabling insertions at specific genomic locations [1][3].\n\n- **Hype outpaces evidence:** Headlines claim CRISPR could treat many diseases and fully eliminated HIV [4][5]. The HIV result is from cell culture, so it does not establish clinical efficacy [5]."}

